Below is a complete, step‑by‑step recipe for getting a **`.pmtiles`** archive onto a MapLibre GL JS map.  
It works with the current MapLibre GL JS (≥ 2.4.0) and the official **PMTiles** JavaScript library (the same code that powers the Protomaps viewer).  

---

## 1. What you need

| Item | Why it matters |
|------|----------------|
| **MapLibre GL JS** | The map engine that will render the tiles. |
| **PMTiles JS library** (`@protomaps/pmtiles`) | Provides a custom URL protocol (`pmtiles://`) that MapLibre can understand. |
| **A `.pmtiles` file** | The vector (or raster) tile archive you want to display. It must be reachable via HTTP(S) (or be bundled with your app). |
| **A web server** (or a local dev server) | Browsers won’t load a file directly from the file‑system due to CORS/security restrictions. |

> **Tip:** If you are using a bundler (Webpack, Vite, Parcel, etc.) you can install the library from npm. If you prefer a quick demo you can also load it from a CDN with a `<script>` tag.

---

## 2. Install / import the PMTiles library

### 2‑a. Using npm / Yarn / pnpm (recommended for a real project)

```bash
npm install maplibre-gl @protomaps/pmtiles
# or
yarn add maplibre-gl @protomaps/pmtiles
```

### 2‑b. Using a CDN (quick demo)

```html
<!-- MapLibre GL JS -->
<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@latest/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
<link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@latest/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />

<!-- PMTiles library -->
<script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@protomaps/pmtiles@latest/dist/index.min.js"></script>
```

> The CDN version exposes a global `PMTiles` constructor, so you can skip the `import` statement.

---

## 3. Register the `pmtiles://` protocol with MapLibre

MapLibre only knows about `http://`, `https://`, `data:` and a few other built‑ins.  
The PMTiles library adds a **custom protocol handler** that tells MapLibre how to fetch a tile from inside the archive.

### 3‑a. ES‑module / bundler style

```js
import maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
import { PMTiles } from '@protomaps/pmtiles';

// 1️⃣ Create a PMTiles object that points at your archive.
//    It can be a remote URL or a relative path on the same domain.
const pmtiles = new PMTiles('https://example.com/tiles.pmtiles');

// 2️⃣ Register the protocol with MapLibre.
maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', (request) => {
  // `request` is an object { url, ... } – we just forward it to the PMTiles instance.
  return pmtiles.get(request);
});
```

### 3‑b. CDN / script‑tag style (no bundler)

```html
<script>
  // `PMTiles` is already on the global scope because we loaded the CDN script.
  const pmtiles = new PMTiles('https://example.com/tiles.pmtiles');

  // MapLibre is also global (`maplibregl`).
  maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', (request) => pmtiles.get(request));
</script>
```

> **What `addProtocol` does:**  
> When MapLibre sees a source URL that starts with `pmtiles://`, it calls the handler you supplied. The handler returns a **Promise** that resolves to an object `{ data: ArrayBuffer, cacheControl?: string, expires?: string }`. The PMTiles library knows how to read the correct slice of the archive and return the raw tile bytes, so MapLibre can decode them exactly as if they had come from a normal XYZ endpoint.

---

## 4. Create the map

```js
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',          // id of the <div> in your HTML
  style: {
    version: 8,
    sources: {},             // we’ll add sources later
    layers: []               // and layers later
  },
  center: [0, 0],            // lon, lat
  zoom: 2
});
```

You can also start from a standard style URL (e.g. a MapLibre “basic” style) and then add your own source on top of it.

---

## 5. Add the `.pmtiles` archive as a **source**

### 5‑a. Vector tiles (most common)

```js
map.addSource('my-pmtiles-source', {
  type: 'vector',
  // The URL must use the custom protocol we registered.
  // The part after the protocol is just an identifier – it can be anything.
  // The library ignores it; it only cares that the protocol is "pmtiles".
  url: 'pmtiles://tiles.pmtiles',
  // Optional: you can hint the min/max zoom if you know them.
  // minzoom: 0,
  // maxzoom: 14,
});
```

### 5‑b. Raster tiles (if your archive contains raster PNG/JPEG tiles)

```js
map.addSource('my-pmtiles-raster', {
  type: 'raster',
  url: 'pmtiles://tiles.pmtiles',
  tileSize: 256,          // or 512 – whatever your archive uses
  // optional: minzoom / maxzoom
});
```

> **Why the URL looks weird:**  
> The string after `pmtiles://` is *not* a real network address. It’s just a token that the protocol handler receives. The handler (`pmtiles.get`) ignores the token and always reads from the `PMTiles` instance you created earlier. This means you can register **multiple** archives by creating multiple `PMTiles` objects and returning the appropriate one based on the token, e.g.:

```js
const archives = {
  'world': new PMTiles('https://example.com/world.pmtiles'),
  'roads': new PMTiles('https://example.com/roads.pmtiles')
};

maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', (request) => {
  const token = request.url.split('://')[1]; // e.g. "world"
  const archive = archives[token];
  return archive ? archive.get(request) : null;
});
```

---

## 6. Add a layer that uses the source

You need to know the **source‑layer name** that lives inside the vector tile archive.  
You can discover it in three ways:

1. **Inspect the archive** with the Protomaps viewer (`https://viewer.protomaps.com/`) – it lists all layers.  
2. **Read the `metadata.json`** that many PMTiles creators embed.  
3. **Guess** (common names are `roads`, `water`, `landuse`, etc.) and look at the console for “source‑layer not found” errors.

Assuming the layer you want is called `roads`:

```js
map.addLayer({
  id: 'roads',
  type: 'line',
  source: 'my-pmtiles-source',
  'source-layer': 'roads',   // <-- exact name inside the .pmtiles
  paint: {
    'line-color': '#ff6600',
    'line-width': 2
  }
});
```

If you’re using a raster source, the layer is simpler:

```js
map.addLayer({
  id: 'pmtiles-raster',
  type: 'raster',
  source: 'my-pmtiles-raster',
  paint: {}
});
```

---

## 7. Full working example (HTML + inline script)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>MapLibre + PMTiles demo</title>
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@latest/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet"/>
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; padding:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<!-- MapLibre GL JS -->
<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@latest/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>

<!-- PMTiles library -->
<script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@protomaps/pmtiles@latest/dist/index.min.js"></script>

<script>
  // 1️⃣ Register the protocol
  const pmtiles = new PMTiles('https://raw.githubusercontent.com/protomaps/PMTiles/master/examples/data/nyc.pmtiles');
  maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', (request) => pmtiles.get(request));

  // 2️⃣ Initialise the map
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {},
      layers: []
    },
    center: [-73.9857, 40.7484], // NYC
    zoom: 12
  });

  // 3️⃣ Add the vector source
  map.on('load', () => {
    map.addSource('nyc-pmtiles', {
      type: 'vector',
      url: 'pmtiles://nyc.pmtiles'   // token can be anything
    });

    // 4️⃣ Add a layer – we know the source‑layer is called "building"
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'buildings',
      type: 'fill',
      source: 'nyc-pmtiles',
      'source-layer': 'building',
      paint: {
        'fill-color': '#6c5ce7',
        'fill-opacity': 0.5
      }
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

*What this does*  

* Loads the `nyc.pmtiles` archive from GitHub (publicly reachable).  
* Registers the `pmtiles://` protocol.  
* Adds a vector source that points at that archive.  
* Draws the `building` layer from the archive as a semi‑transparent fill.

Open the file in a browser (served via a local dev server, e.g. `python -m http.server`) and you should see the NYC building footprints rendered instantly—no network requests for individual tiles after the first few byte‑range fetches.

---

## 8. Advanced tips & gotchas

| Issue | How to solve / avoid |
|-------|----------------------|
| **CORS errors** when the `.pmtiles` file lives on another domain | The PMTiles library uses **HTTP Range requests**. The remote server must send `Access-Control-Allow-Origin:*` (or your origin) on **both the initial HEAD/GET** and on the **206 Partial Content** responses. If you control the server, add the header. If you can’t, proxy the file through a server you control. |
| **Large archives cause a long initial HEAD request** | The library only needs the archive’s *footer* (last 100 KB) to read the index. It automatically issues a `Range: bytes=-100000` request. Make sure your server supports range requests. |
| **Multiple archives in the same map** | Create a separate `PMTiles` instance for each archive and dispatch based on the token after `pmtiles://`. Example in section 5‑b. |
| **Using a local file during development** | Browsers block `file://` range requests. Run a local HTTP server (`npm i -g serve && serve .` or `python -m http.server`). |
| **Performance** – you can enable the built‑in **cache** of the PMTiles library: | ```js\nconst pmtiles = new PMTiles('url', { maxCacheSize: 256 * 1024 * 1024 }); // 256 MiB cache\n``` |
| **Raster vs. Vector** – the same archive can contain both. Just add two sources (type `raster` and `vector`) that point at the same `pmtiles://` URL. |
| **Styling vector tiles** – you still need to know the layer names and the geometry type (line, fill, circle, symbol, etc.). The PMTiles spec does **not** embed style information; you style them exactly like any other vector source. |
| **Debugging**

