## TL;DR  
`map.addProtocol()` still exists in **MapLibre GL JS v4**, but its callback signature and return contract changed:

| v3 (old) | v4 (new) |
|----------|----------|
| `addProtocol(name, (params) => data)` – can return `ArrayBuffer`, `string`, `Blob`, `Promise<…>` or `null` | `addProtocol(name, (params, abortSignal) => Promise<ProtocolResponse>)` – **must** return a **Promise** that resolves to an object `{ data, cacheControl?, expires?, headers? }`. |
| No built‑in abort handling | The second argument (`abortSignal`) is an `AbortSignal` you should pass to `fetch` (or any async work) so the request can be cancelled when the map discards the tile. |
| Synchronous returns were allowed | Synchronous returns are **no longer accepted** – even if you have the data already you must wrap it in `Promise.resolve({data})`. |

Because of those changes your old callback (which probably returned a raw `ArrayBuffer` or a plain `Promise<ArrayBuffer>`) is silently ignored, and the map falls back to the default “no‑tile” behavior.

Below is a step‑by‑step guide to migrate your custom‑protocol tile loader to v4, with a complete working example.

---

## 1. What the new callback receives

```ts
type ProtocolParameters = {
  // The full URL that was requested (including your custom scheme)
  url: string;

  // The original request method – always "GET" for tiles
  method: string;

  // Any request headers that the map wants to send (e.g. authentication)
  // You can add/override them if you need to.
  headers: Record<string, string>;

  // The map’s current pixel ratio (1, 2, 3 …) – useful for retina tiles
  // (optional, only present for raster sources)
  pixelRatio?: number;

  // The tile’s zoom, x, y (if the URL contains {z}/{x}/{y})
  // These are parsed from the URL and provided for convenience.
  zoom?: number;
  x?: number;
  y?: number;
};

type ProtocolResponse = {
  // The binary payload that the map will treat as a tile.
  // Must be an ArrayBuffer, Uint8Array, Blob, or a string (for vector JSON).
  data: ArrayBuffer | Uint8Array | Blob | string;

  // Optional HTTP‑style cache hints – the map will respect them.
  cacheControl?: string;   // e.g. "max-age=3600"
  expires?: string;        // e.g. new Date().toUTCString()
  // Optional extra response headers (rarely needed)
  headers?: Record<string, string>;
};
```

The **second argument** is an `AbortSignal` that you should forward to any `fetch`/XHR you perform. If the map decides the tile is no longer needed (e.g. the user panned away), it will abort the request.

---

## 2. Minimal migration pattern

```js
// ---------------------------------------------------
// 1️⃣  Register the protocol *before* you create the map
// ---------------------------------------------------
maplibregl.addProtocol('mytiles', async (params, abortSignal) => {
  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣  Build the real URL you want to fetch.
  // -------------------------------------------------
  // Example: mytiles://myserver.com/tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf
  const realUrl = params.url.replace(/^mytiles:\/\//, 'https://myserver.com/tiles/');

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 3️⃣  Perform the request – forward abortSignal.
  // -------------------------------------------------
  const response = await fetch(realUrl, {
    method: params.method,
    headers: params.headers,
    signal: abortSignal,          // <-- crucial for cancellation
    // You can also add credentials, mode, etc. here.
  });

  if (!response.ok) {
    // Throwing an error will make the map treat the tile as “failed”.
    throw new Error(`Tile request failed ${response.status}`);
  }

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 4️⃣  Convert to the type the map expects.
  // -------------------------------------------------
  const arrayBuffer = await response.arrayBuffer();

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 5️⃣  Return the *ProtocolResponse* object.
  // -------------------------------------------------
  return {
    data: arrayBuffer,
    // Optional: let the map cache the tile according to your policy.
    // cacheControl: response.headers.get('Cache-Control') ?? undefined,
    // expires: response.headers.get('Expires') ?? undefined,
  };
});
```

### Why each step matters

| Step | What changed in v4? | What you must do |
|------|--------------------|------------------|
| **1️⃣ Register early** | No change, but keep it *before* `new maplibregl.Map()` so the map can resolve the protocol during source creation. | Same as before. |
| **2️⃣ Build URL** | No change. | Same as before – just make sure you strip the custom scheme (`mytiles://`). |
| **3️⃣ Abort handling** | New `abortSignal` argument. | Pass `signal: abortSignal` to `fetch` (or XHR). If you use a library that doesn’t accept an `AbortSignal`, you’ll need to manually abort or ignore the signal. |
| **4️⃣ Return type** | Must be a **Promise** that resolves to an **object** (`ProtocolResponse`). | Wrap your data in `{ data: … }`. Even if you already have the `ArrayBuffer`, do `return Promise.resolve({ data: myBuffer })`. |
| **5️⃣ Cache hints** | Optional but useful. | You can forward the server’s `Cache‑Control` / `Expires` headers if you want the map’s internal tile cache to respect them. |

---

## 3. Full working example (vector tiles)

Below is a self‑contained snippet you can paste into a page that already loads `maplibre-gl.js` v4. It demonstrates a custom protocol that serves **Mapbox Vector Tiles** (`.pbf`) from a private endpoint.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre v4 – Custom protocol demo</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body { margin:0; padding:0; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<script>
  // -------------------------------------------------
  // Register the protocol (must happen before map creation)
  // -------------------------------------------------
  maplibregl.addProtocol('mytiles', async (params, abortSignal) => {
    // Example URL: mytiles://tiles.example.com/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf
    const realUrl = params.url
      .replace(/^mytiles:\/\//, 'https://tiles.example.com/')   // strip scheme
      .replace('{z}', params.zoom ?? '{z}')
      .replace('{x}', params.x ?? '{x}')
      .replace('{y}', params.y ?? '{y}');

    const response = await fetch(realUrl, {
      method: params.method,
      headers: params.headers,
      signal: abortSignal,
    });

    if (!response.ok) {
      // Let the map know the tile failed – it will show a “missing tile” placeholder.
      throw new Error(`Tile fetch error ${response.status}`);
    }

    const arrayBuffer = await response.arrayBuffer();

    // Forward cache headers (optional but recommended)
    const cacheControl = response.headers.get('Cache-Control') || undefined;
    const expires = response.headers.get('Expires') || undefined;

    return {
      data: arrayBuffer,
      cacheControl,
      expires,
    };
  });

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // Create the map
  // -------------------------------------------------
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {
        // Use the custom protocol in the source URL
        mySource: {
          type: 'vector',
          // Note the "mytiles://" scheme – the map will invoke the handler above.
          url: 'mytiles://tiles.example.com/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf',
          // If you need to pass query params (e.g. token) you can embed them:
          // url: 'mytiles://tiles.example.com/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf?access_token=YOUR_TOKEN'
        }
      },
      layers: [
        {
          id: 'my-layer',
          type: 'fill',
          source: 'mySource',
          'source-layer': 'my_layer_name', // adjust to your vector source name
          paint: {
            'fill-color': '#ff6600',
            'fill-opacity': 0.5
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    center: [0, 0],
    zoom: 2
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

**What to check if it still doesn’t work**

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| No tiles appear, console shows `Error: Tile request failed 404` | URL built incorrectly (e.g. missing `{z}/{x}/{y}`) | Log `realUrl` before `fetch` and verify it matches a working endpoint. |
| Tiles load but then disappear when panning quickly | Abort signal not forwarded → request cancelled before `fetch` resolves | Ensure `signal: abortSignal` is passed to `fetch`. |
| Map logs `Protocol handler for "mytiles" returned a non‑Promise` | You returned raw data instead of an object or Promise | Wrap in `Promise.resolve({data: …})`. |
| “Failed to load source” error on map init | Protocol registered *after* map creation | Move `addProtocol` call **before** `new maplibregl.Map`. |
| Tiles load but are never cached (re‑download on every pan) | No cache headers returned | Return `cacheControl` / `expires` from the server or set them manually in the response object. |

---

## 4. Common pitfalls & how to avoid them

| Pitfall | Why it broke after v3→v4 | How to fix |
|---------|------------------------|------------|
| **Returning a plain `ArrayBuffer`** | v3 accepted it; v4 expects an object. | `return Promise.resolve({ data: myArrayBuffer });` |
| **Using `XMLHttpRequest` without abort support** | v4 passes an `AbortSignal`. Without it the request can’t be cancelled, leading to “stale tile” warnings. | Switch to `fetch` (or a library that accepts `signal`). If you must stay with XHR, listen to `abortSignal.addEventListener('abort', ...)` and call `xhr.abort()`. |
| **Forgot to forward request headers** | Some services require `Authorization` or `User-Agent`. v3 silently ignored missing headers; v4 still forwards them but you must copy them if you build a new request. | `headers: params.headers` in the `fetch` options. |
| **Using `map.addProtocol` instead of the static `maplibregl.addProtocol`** | Both still exist, but the static version is recommended because the map instance may not be ready when the source is parsed. | Use `maplibregl.addProtocol(...)` (or `map.addProtocol` *after* the map is constructed, but before any source that uses the protocol is added). |
| **Assuming `params` contains `url` only** | v4 adds `method`, `headers`, `pixelRatio`, `zoom/x/y` etc. |
